[[File:Tsukubasan-jinja ōtorii.JPG|thumb|right|200px|{{Center|Ōtorii}}]]'''Tsukubasan Shrine'' (Tsukubasan Shrine) is located in {{ill|lt=Ibaraki Prefecture|Q83273|WD=Q83273|1=Ibaraki Prefecture}}{{ill|lt=Tsukuba City|Q273863|WD=Q273863|1=Tsukuba}}{{ill|lt=Tsukuba (Tsukuba City)|Q11602837|WD=Q11602837|lt=Tsukuba|1=Tsukuba}}. {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinai Shrine|1=Shikinaisha|WD=Q134917286|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|WD=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}} 1 shrine, 1 small shrine). {{ill|lt=Modern shrine rank system|Q712534|WD=Q712534|lt=Old shrine rank|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} is a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=prefectural shrine|1=Ken-sha|WD=Q90139226}}, and currently it is {{ill|lt=Beppon Shrine|Q10898274|WD=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of {{ill|lt=Shrine Headquarters|Q908077|WD=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

=== Overview ===
It is a shrine that enshrines {{ill|lt=Mt. Tsukuba|Q1754619|WD=Q1754619|1=Mount Tsukuba}}, which rises to the southwest of {{ill|lt=Ibaraki Prefecture|Q83273|WD=Q83273|1=Ibaraki Prefecture}} in the northeastern part of the {{ill|lt=Kanto Plain|Q1124865|WD=Q1124865|1=Kantō Plain}}, as {{ill|lt=Shintaiyama|Q1595289|WD=Q1595289|1=Sacred mountains}}, and the main shrine buildings are located in the following three locations.
:* '''Mt. Nantai Main Hall'' - The summit of the west peak (Mt. Nantai, 871 meters above sea level).
:* '''Mt. Nyotai Main Hall''' - The summit of the eastern peak (Mt. Nyotai, 877 meters above sea level.<ref group="Note">The elevation of Mt. Nyotai was revised from 876 meters in 1999.</ref>).
:* '''Hotel'''' - Mountainside (270 meters above sea level). View both main shrines from a distance.
Mt. Tsukuba is a double-eared peak consisting of a west peak and an east peak, and the main shrine of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine is located on each peak. The precincts are vast, with the shrine site extending over the 270 meter above sea level line (prayer hall) on the south side of Mt. Tsukuba, and its area is approximately 354 {{ill|lt=ha|Q35852|WD=Q35852|1=Hectare}}<ref name="Yoshisho"/>.

A {{ill|lt=monzen town|Q10564515|WD=Q10564515|1=Monzen-machi}} is formed around the worship hall halfway up the south side. Generally speaking, ``Tsukubasan Shrine'' often refers to the area around this worship hall. To the west of the shrine is Miyawaki Station of {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Scenic Railway|Q11602894|WD=Q11602894|1=Tsukuba Scenic Railway}}'s {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Scenic Railway Tsukuba Cable Railway Line|Q6924212|WD=Q6924212|lt=Mt. Tsukuba Cable Car|1=Mount Tsukuba Cable Car}}, and there is also a trailhead, which is crowded with not only worshipers but also mountain climbers.Mt. Tsukuba has been deeply worshiped as a divine mountain ever since it appeared in ``{{ill|lt=Hitachikuni Fudoki|Q11481687|WD=Q11481687|1=Hitachi no Kuni Fudoki}},'' and Mt. Tsukuba Shrine, which enshrines the divine spirit, was deeply revered by court nobles and samurai families. As for cultural properties, the long sword (Mei Yoshimune) has been designated as a national {{ill|lt=Important Cultural Property|Q1188622|WD=Q1188622|1=Important Cultural Property (Japan)}}, and several shrine buildings have been designated as tangible cultural properties by Ibaraki Prefecture and Tsukuba City.

=== Enshrined God ===
The main {{ill|lt=enshrined deity|Q11591100|WD=Q11591100|1=Saijin}} is the following two pillars:
:* '''Tsukuba Otoko no Kami'' (Tsukuba Otokami, Tsukuba Otokami)
:*: The god of Mt. Nantai. The personal god is '''{{ill|lt=Izanagi|Q813858|WD=Q813858|lt=Izanagi no Mikoto|1=Izanagi}}''' (Izanagi no Mikoto).
:* '''Tsukuba Goddess''' (Tsukuba Goddess)
:*: God of Mount Nyotai. The personal god is '''{{ill|lt=Izanami|Q682306|WD=Q682306|lt=Izanami no Mikoto|1=Izanami}}'''.

The {{ill|lt=Shintai|Q327532|WD=Q327532|1=Shintai}} of the main shrine is a divine robe (kanmiso), which is replaced at the Gozakae Festival {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Official Website)|Tsukubasan Shrine History}}. In addition, in "{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Meiji Shrine Journals|1=UNKNOWN}}" it is referred to as a divine mirror {{Sfn|Meiji Shrine Journals|1912}}.

Since the two gods Izanago no Mikoto and Iza Shokuson got married and gave birth to many gods and domestic births, they are believed to have blessings such as matchmaking, marital harmony, family safety, child-bearing, and child rearing. It is also said to have blessings such as traffic safety and construction safety.

=== About the enshrined deity ===
Mt. Tsukuba is a double-eared peak consisting of Mt. Nantai and Mt. Nyotai, each of which is dedicated to a deity. It is said that because of the mountain's appearance, with its two peaks side by side, it was natural for two male and female ancestor deities to be enshrined here.

"{{ill|lt=Hitachi Province Fudoki|Q11481687|WD=Q11481687|1=Hitachi no Kuni Fudoki}}" <ref group="Hara" name="Fudoki">"Hitachi Province Fudoki" Tsukuba-gunjo (see {{Harvnb|Shinto and Shrine Historical Materials Collection}}). Regarding Mt. Tsukuba, </ref> writes that the west peak (Mt. Nantai) was so steep that climbing it was prohibited as it was considered a divine peak. On the other hand, Higashimine (Mt. Nyotai) is steep, but springs flow constantly in summer and winter, and it is said that men and women gathered there in spring and autumn to perform {{ill|lt=utagaki|Q610915|WD=Q610915|1=Utagaki}}. In this way, Mt. Nantai was considered a restricted area, while Mt. Nyotai was considered an open area, and at the time the Fudoki was compiled, their characteristics were already very different. Numerous ritual artifacts have also been discovered near Mt. Nyotai from the end of the 7th century to the 12th century {{Sfn|Mt. Tsukuba (Kadokawa)|1983}}.

The name of the god is written in {{ill|lt=Rokukokushi|Q1151168|WD=Q1151168|1=Rikkokushi}} as ``Tsukuba male god'' and ``Tsukuba goddess.'' In the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|WD=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, there were some theories that posited a personal god, and the following description can be found in the literature:{{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukubasan Shrine|1984|p=414}}* Ichishasha {{ill|lt=Yamato Takeru|Q461258|WD=Q461258|lt=Nihon Takeru|1=Yamato Takeru}}, Ichishasha {{ill|lt=younger Tachibanahime|Q11487396|WD=Q11487396|lt=younger Tachibanahime|1=Tachibanahime-no-Mikoto}}, commonly known as Yin-Yang Two Pillars (``Jingi Hoden'') {{Sfn|Kitabatake Mt. Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}
* {{ill|lt=Izanagi|Q813858|WD=Q813858|lt=Izanagison|1=Izanagi}} Zainmine, {{ill|lt=Izanami|Q682306|WD=Q682306|lt=Izanagison|1=Izanami}}zainmine (“Hitachikuni 28 Shrines”) {{Sfn|Kitabatake Mt. Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}
* Yomine{{ill|lt=Hannyas|Q9285919|WD=Q9285919|lt=Haniyama Hikokami|1=Haniyasu-hiko and Haniyasu-hime}}, Inmine{{ill|lt=Hannyas|Q9285919|WD=Q9285919|lt=Haniyama Himekami|1=Haniyasu-hiko and Haniyasu-hime}} ("{{ill|lt=Divine Name Book Research|Q11588824|WD=Q11588824|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō Studies}}") {{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}
* The enshrined deity is unknown, and legend says, ``Izanagison Zaiyomine, Izashoson Zainmine, commonly known as Tsukuba Daimyojin'' ({{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}'12 ({{ill|lt=1879|Q7783|WD=Q7783|1=1879}}) ``Mount Tsukuba Shrine Detailed Record'') {{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}After that, based on the records in the Six Kingdoms History, in January 1942 ({{ill|lt=1909|Q2057|WD=Q2057|1=1909}}), the names of the deities enshrined were determined to be ``Tsukuba Otokojin'' and ``Tsukuba Goddess.''{{Sfn|Meiji Shrine Magazine Materials|1912}}. Then, in {{ill|lt=Taisho|Q329174|WD=Q329174|1=Taishō era}}11 ({{ill|lt=1922|Q2165|WD=Q2165|1=1922}}), it was decided that ``Tsukuba Man God/Tsukuba Goddess'' should be written with ``Iza Nōson/Iza Shoson.''{{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}. Furthermore, the origin and history of the tradition of Izaninoson and Izashozon is not clear. 

=== History ===

=== Founding ===
Founding date is unknown. Mount Tsukuba is said to have been revered since the time when people began living in the Kanto Plain, as it is so impressive that it is contrasted with {{ill|lt=Mt. Fuji|Q39231|WD=Q39231|1=Mount Fuji}} in the ``{{ill|lt=Hitachikuni Fudoki|Q11481687|WD=Q11481687|1=Hitachi no Kuni Fudoki}}''.

According to the shrine biography (``Mt. Tsukuba Engi''), {{jalink|domestic production|Q3545163} written by Iza Nōson and Iza Shokuson in {{ill|lt=Kojiki|Q813031|WD=Q813031|1=Kojiki}}. }} "{{ill|lt=Onogoro Island|Q2985158|WD=Q2985158|lt=Onokoro Island|1=Onogoro Island}}" is said to correspond to Mt. Tsukuba {{Sfn|Mt. Tsukuba Shrine (Kadokawa)|1983}}. In addition, in "Shiririn Kayosho" ({{ill|lt=Nanbokucho Period (Japan)|Q264012|WD=Q264012|lt=Nanbokucho Period|1=Nanboku-chō period}}), Mt. Tsukuba is said to be the sacred mountain where {{ill|lt=Amaterasu Omikami|UNKNOWN|lt=Amaterasu Omikami}} descended. (era name)|Q1373146|Culture}} ({{ill|lt=1804|Q6894|WD=Q6894|1=1804}}-{{ill|lt=1818|Q6964|WD=Q6964|1=1818}}) "Kiji" also tells about the transfer of the same god from Tsukuba to Ise.Regarding the ritual clans, since Tsukuba no Mikoto (Tsukuba Mikoto, Tsukuba Mikoto), who appears in "Hitachi no Kuni Fudoki", was appointed as the first {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Kunizo|Q11602846|WD=Q11602846|1=Tsukuba Kunizo}}<ref group="note" name="Kunizo"/>, it is said that Tsukuba Kunizo served in unison with rituals and politics<ref name="historical history book"/><ref group="Note">The founding tradition of the nearby {{ill|lt=Serikage Shrine|Q17228464|WD=Q17228464|1=Kokage Shrine}} (Kami-gun, Tsukuba City) also says that during the reign of {{ill|lt=Emperor Seimu|Q316672|WD=Q316672|1=Emperor Seimu}}, Tsukuba Kunizo's Akishiro-no-Mikoto enshrined Tsukuba Okami. </ref>. Many ancient tombs remain at the foot of Mt. Tsukuba, among which {{ill|lt=Hachimanzuka Tumulus (Tsukuba City)|Q17218702|WD=Q17218702|lt=Hachimanzuka Tumulus|1=Hachimanzuka-kofun}} (Numata, Tsukuba City) is thought to be a Tsukuba Kuni-zukuri tomb.{{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=414}}.

The current main part of Tsukubasan Shrine is located around the worship hall, but the worship hall was built in 1875 on the site of Chuzenji Temple. Therefore, the "original Mt. Tsukuba Shrine" refers to the "Nantai Mt. Shrine and Nyotai Mt. Shrine". Tsukuba Shrine|1984|p=412}}. </ref>. Also, although the date of its establishment is unknown, the ruins of Rokusho Shrine in Usui, Tsukuba City (currently a closed shrine) were the satomiya of both shrines (see section {{ill|lt=#Related locations|UNKNOWN|lt=Related locations}}).

=== Outline ===

==== From the Nara period to the Heian period ====
``{{ill|lt=Manyoshu|Q834321|WD=Q834321|1=Man'yōshū}}'' from the {{ill|lt=Nara period|Q189178|WD=Q189178|1=Nara period}} includes 25 poems from Tsukuba, and Mt. Tsukuba is said to have been the mountain representing {{ill|lt=Hitachi Province|Q1055353|WD=Q1055353|1=Hitachi Province}}. Also, from the end of the {{ill|lt=Nara period|Q189178|WD=Q189178|1=Nara period}} to the beginning of the {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|WD=Q193292|1=Heian period}}, the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Hosso sect|1=UNKNOWN}} The monk {{ill|lt=Tokuichi|Q7813728|WD=Q7813728|1=Tokuitsu}} opened Tsukubasanji Temple, later Tsukubasan Chisokuin Chuzenji Temple {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. As a result, {{ill|lt=Shinto-Buddhist syncretism|Q2279088|WD=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} progressed, and Mt. Tsukuba developed into one of the leading {{ill|lt=Shugendo|Q1483875|WD=Q1483875|1=Shugendō}} dojos {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}} (for details, see the {{ill|lt=#Chuzenji|UNKNOWN|lt=Chuzenji}} section). During this period of syncretization of Shinto and Buddhism, it was also called ``Tsukuba Ryodaigongen'' (Ryobe Gongen).In the {{ill|lt=Rokukokushi|Q1151168|WD=Q1151168|lt=Kokushi|1=Rikkokushi}}, there is an article that says that in ancient times, in {{ill|lt=Honin|Q1373070|WD=Q1373070|1=Kōnin (era)}} 14 ({{ill|lt=823|Q29944|WD=Q29944|1=823}}) <ref group="hara" name="Honin 14"/>, the Tsukuba deity, which was lower than the Junior Fifth Rank, was made {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=government shrine|1=UNKNOWN}}. With the subsequent investiture of {{ill|lt=Divine Rank|Q11591025|WD=Q11591025|1=Shinkai (divine rank)}}, {{ill|lt=Jōkan (Japan)|Q1075635|WD=Q1075635|lt=Jōkan|1=Jōgan}}13 ({{ill|lt=871|Q24754|WD=Q24754|1=871}})<ref group="Hara" In name="Jōgan 13"/>, Tsukuba Ogami rose to Jusanmi (Junior Third Rank), and in Jogan 16 ({{ill|lt=874|Q23843|WD=Q23843|1=874}}) <ref group="Hara" name="Jōgan 16"/>, Tsukuba Goddess rose to Jushini-Ji (or Sei-shii Shimi-ge <{{ill|lt=#Divine rank|UNKNOWN|lt=described later}}>).{{ill|lt=Extension (era name)|Q1327274|WD=Q1327274|lt=Extension|1=Enchō}} "{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|WD=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}" established in 5 years ({{ill|lt=927|Q30470|WD=Q30470|1=927}}) Tsukuba District, Hitachi Province. </ref>In {{ill|lt=Hitachi-kuni|Q1055353|WD=Q1055353|1=Hitachi Province}}{{ill|lt=Tsukuba-gun|Q7850092|WD=Q7850092|1=Tsukuba District, Ibaraki}} there are "Tsukubasan Shrine Niza Ichiza Meishindai It is listed as the only {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=shikinaisha|1=UNKNOWN}} shrine in Tsukuba County, of which {{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|WD=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}} is said to be the Tsukuba Man God, and the small shrine is said to be the Tsukuba Goddess {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. After that, in {{ill|lt=Jisho|Q1194671|WD=Q1194671|1=Jishō}} 4th year ({{ill|lt=1180|Q19702|WD=Q19702|1=1180}}), <ref name="Yamakawa"/> is said to have reached the rank of Shoichi.

==== From Kamakura period to Edo period ====
In the early {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|WD=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}}, {{ill|lt=Tomoie Hatta|Q11391665|WD=Q11391665|1=Tomoie Hatta}} of {{ill|lt=Hitachi Province|Q1055353|WD=Q1055353|1=Hitachi Province}}{{ill|lt=Guo|Q1141023|WD=Q1141023|1=Shugo}} ({{ill|lt=Oda clan|Q11463150|WD=Q11463150|1=Oda clan}}'s son Hachiro Tameji inherited the name of Tsukuba Kunizo and became Tsukuba {{ill|lt=Betto|Q2900274|WD=Q2900274|1=Bettō}} {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. The descendants of this Tameji Tsukuba (Myogen) thereafter called themselves the Tsukuba clan, and were the enshriners of the Tsukuba deity as well as the charge of Chuzenji temple {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. The trends in the Middle Ages are not clear, but in {{ill|lt=Keicho|Q1328185|WD=Q1328185|1=Keichō}}5 ({{ill|lt=1600|Q6720|WD=Q6720|1=1600}}) Until the Tsukuba clan was removed by Kawaieyasu|Q171977}}, the Tsukuba deity, Chuzenji, is said to have been under the control of the Tsukuba clan {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Kadokawa)|1983}}.

In the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|WD=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, the {{ill|lt=Edo Shogunate|Q205662|WD=Q205662|lt=Bakufu|1=Tokugawa shogunate}} fiercely protected Chuzenji, many temples and pagodas were erected within the grounds of Chuzenji, and the character of Mt. Tsukuba changed from a co-existence of Shinto and Buddhism to a sacred place centered on Buddhism.{{Sfn|Mount Tsukuba Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. Despite this patronage of Chuzen-ji Temple, the shadow of Tsukuba-kami was weak, and the form of Tsukuba-kami's enshrinement during this period is not clear {{Sfn|Mt. Tsukuba Shrine (National History)|1988}} (for trends in the Edo period, see section {{ill|lt=#Chuzenji|UNKNOWN|lt=Chuzenji}}).At the end of the Edo period, the Tenguto raised an army in the first year of {{ill|lt=Genji|Q1325419|WD=Q1325419|1=Genji era}} ({{ill|lt=1864|Q7704|WD=Q7704|1=1864}}), and {{ja link|Tenguto Rebellion|Q1040831}} ({{ill|lt=Tenguto Rebellion#Mt.Tsukuba Raising of Arms|Q1040831|WD=Q1040831|lt=Mt. Tsukuba Incident|1=Mito Rebellion}}).

