</div>{{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}} After the Meiji Restoration, Chuzenji was abolished due to {{ill|lt=Separation of Shinto and Buddhism|Q1970535|WD=Q1970535|1=Shinbutsu bunri}} {{Sfn|Mt. Tsukuba Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. As a result, much of the temple was destroyed, but only a few buildings survived and remain today as cultural properties. Also, due to this separation of Buddhism and Shintoism, Tsukuba-san Shrine was rebuilt, and its main part was built on the site of Chuzen-ji Temple, and it still exists today.

* In September of the 2nd year of the Meiji era ({{ill|lt=1869|Q7720|WD=Q7720|1=1869}}), {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (official site)|Tsukubasan Shrine history}}, with Modayoshi Shirakawa of {{ill|lt=Shirakawa Royal Family|Q11579697|WD=Q11579697|1=Shirakawa family}} as its priest.
* On October 4, 1873 (1873|Q7757}}, it was ranked as {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=prefectural shrine|1=Ken-sha|WD=Q90139226}} in the {{ill|lt=modern shrine ranking system|Q712534|WD=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (official website)|Tsukubasan Shrine history}}.
* In the 8th year of Meiji ({{ill|lt=1875|Q7764|WD=Q7764|1=1875}}), a worship hall was built on the site of the Chuzenji main hall (Omido) {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}.
* On September 4, 1908 (Meiji 41) ({{ill|lt=1908|Q2056|WD=Q2056|1=1908}}), 84 precinct shrines were enshrined at Kasuga Shrine due to shrine reorganization.{{Sfn|Meiji Shrine Journal |1912}}.
* After the war, it was ranked as {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|WD=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of {{ill|lt=Shinja Honcho|Q908077|WD=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.* In {{ill|lt=Showa|Q327567|WD=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}}30 ({{ill|lt=1955|Q18577|WD=Q18577|1=1955}}), the Nantaiyama main shrine was renovated (the current main shrine)<ref name="haiden">A record of the construction history of the worship hall. </ref>.
* In 1979 ({{ill|lt=1979|Q2484|WD=Q2484|1=1979}}), the Nyotaiyama main hall was renovated (current main hall) <ref name="haiden"/>.

