=== Precincts ===
The precincts of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine are 354 {{ill|lt=hectare|Q35852|WD=Q35852|1=Hectare}} on the south side of Mt. Tsukuba, more than 270 meters above sea level. </ref>. The main shrine is located on the summit of Mt. Nantai and Mt. Nyotai of Mt. Tsukuba, and there is a shrine on the mountainside where you can worship from a distance.

=== Tsukuba Yamanaka ===
[[File:Tsukubasan-jinja nantaisan-honden.JPG|thumb|180px|right|{{Center|Nantaiyama Main Hall}}]]
[[File:Mount Tsukuba Shrine on top.jpg|thumb|180px|right|{{Center|Nyotaiyama Main Hall}}]]
'''Mt. Nyotai Main Hall'' was built in {{ill|lt=Showa|Q327567|WD=Q327567|1=Shōwa era}}30 ({{ill|lt=1955|Q18577|WD=Q18577|1=1955}}), '''Mt. The main hall''' was renovated in 1979 ({{ill|lt=1979|Q2484|WD=Q2484|1=1979}}), and both are Ikkensha {{ill|lt=Ryuzo|Q210684|WD=Q210684|1=Nagare-zukuri}}. At the summit of Mt. Nantai, there is a joint observatory with {{ill|lt=University of Tsukuba|Q743615|WD=Q743615|1=University of Tsukuba}} {{ill|lt=Joint Meteorological Observatory of Tsukubasan Shrine and University of Tsukuba Computational Science Research Center|Q11602877|WD=Q11602877|1=Meteorological Station of the Mt. Tsukuba Shrine & Center for Computational Sciences, University of Tsukuba}} (facilities and land owned by Tsukubasan Shrine <ref> Hayashi (2007): p. 7. </ref><ref>Nishimura (2010): p. 559. </ref>) At the summit of Mt. Nyotai, there is a shrine awarding place and Ama no Ukihashi (rebuilt in April 2009).

The saddle between the two peaks is called ``Miyukigahara.'' About 300 meters to Mt. Nantai and about 600 meters to Mt. Nyotai. The Tsukuba Cable Car's Tsukuba Sancho Station is located here, and there are many souvenir shops and restaurants.In addition, in the mountains there is also the "Men and Women River," which is famous as the subject of {{ill|lt=Emperor Yozei|Q349280|WD=Q349280|lt=Yoseiin|1=Emperor Yōzei}}'s poem {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Ogura Hyakunin Isshu|1=UNKNOWN}}. (Ibaraki Prefecture) In addition to the source of the Minanogawa River, there is also the Shihosugi Cedar. There are many sacred stones, strangely shaped rocks, and famous trees, such as the {{ill|lt=cedar|Q147388|WD=Q147388|1=Cryptomeria}} named ``Cedar'' (near the headwaters of the Men and Women River), which is estimated to be 800 years old, ``Benkei Seven Returns'', ``Mother's Womb Passing'', and ``Gama Stone''. These rocky areas were once used as a dojo for {{ill|lt=Shugendo|Q1483875|WD=Q1483875|1=Shugendō}}, and Mt. Tsukuba meditation is still performed as a Shinto ritual.

=== Around the shrine ===
[[File:Tsukubasan-jinja zuishinmon.JPG|thumb|160px|right|{{Center|Zuishinmon<br />(Tsukuba City designated cultural property)}}]]
[[File:Tsukubasan Shrine Oshinbashi Front.JPG|thumb|160px|right|{{Center|Shinbashi<br />(Ibaraki Prefecture Designated Cultural Property)}}]]
A {{ill|lt=monzen town|Q10564515|WD=Q10564515|1=Monzen-machi}} is formed around the worship hall, which forms the main part of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine. However, before the Meiji era, this area was a sacred place centered on Buddhism, with Chuzenji as its main temple {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}, but when Chuzenji was abolished after the Meiji Restoration, it was revived as a shrine and a worship hall was built.``The Hall of Worship'' was built in {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}8 ({{ill|lt=1875|Q7764|WD=Q7764|1=1875}}). The plaque on the front, "Tsukuba Shrine," is written by {{ill|lt=Prince Komatsunomiya Akihito|Q712627|WD=Q712627|1=Prince Komatsu Akihito}} and engraved by {{ill|lt=Koun Takamura|Q2080344|WD=Q2080344|1=Takamura Kōun}} from <ref> Famous Places Guide (Mito Transportation Office, 1927), page 3. </ref>. Before the Meiji period, there was the main hall (Omido) of Chuzenji Temple at this location, but after the Omido was abolished due to the separation of Shinto and Buddhism, a new one was built {{Sfn|Tsukubasan Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. The foundation stone remains in front of the worship hall, reminiscent of the Chuzenji temple {{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukubasan Shrine|1984|p=418}}. Furthermore, Omido has been rebuilt as {{ill|lt=Omido (Tsukuba City)|Q11435427|WD=Q11435427|lt=Omido|1=Ōmi-dō}} of the {{ill|lt=Toyoyama School of Shingon Sect|Q3077351|WD=Q3077351|1=Shingon-shū Buzan-ha}} Omido Church to the southwest of the worship hall.The ``Zuishinmon'' (Zuishinmon) built in front of the worship hall is a tower gate with a width of 5 x 2 shaku and a depth of 3 x 3, making it one of the largest in Ibaraki Prefecture. </ref>. It was originally donated by the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu in {{ill|lt=Kanei|Q1192913|WD=Q1192913|1=Kan'ei}}10 ({{ill|lt=1633|Q6870|WD=Q6870|1=1633}}), but {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Horeki|1=UNKNOWN}} It was burnt down in 4th year ({{ill|lt=1754|Q7623|WD=Q7623|1=1754}}), and although it was rebuilt, it was burnt down again in 4th year of Meiwa ({{ill|lt=1767|Q7672|WD=Q7672|1=1767}})<ref name="Zuishinmon"/>. The current tower gate was rebuilt in {{ill|lt=Bunka (era name)|Q1373146|WD=Q1373146|lt=Bunka|1=Bunka}}8 ({{ill|lt=1811|Q6928|WD=Q6928|1=1811}})<ref name="Zuishinmon"/>. During the {{ill|lt=syncretism of Shinto and Buddhism|Q2279088|WD=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} period, Nio statues (Vajra Rikishi statues) were enshrined there as ``Niomon'', but after {{ill|lt=Separation of Shinto and Buddhism|Q1970535|WD=Q1970535|1=Shinbutsu bunri}}, it was called ``Zuishinmon''. During the separation of Shinto and Buddhism, the Nio statue was floated down the Sakuragawa River on a raft and taken to Tofukuji Temple in Matsuzuka, Tsukuba City. For this reason, it is now called ``Nagare Nio.'' <ref>Takayoshi Nishitani. The sacred mountain of Mt. Tsukuba and Tokuichi Daishi: Chisoku-in Chuzenji and Mt. Tsukuba Shrine. Ibaraki Prefecture Local Culture Honor Society, 2012</ref>. At the current Zuishinmon gate, the Zuishin statue of {{ill|lt=Yamato Takeru|Q461258|WD=Q461258|lt=Yamato Takeru no Mikoto|1=Yamato Takeru}} (Yamato Takeru no Mikoto) is enshrined on the left side of the shrine, and on the right side is the Zuishin statue of {{ill|lt=Yamato Takeru|Q461258|WD=Q461258|lt=Yamato Takeru no Mikoto|1=Yamato Takeru}}. This Zuishinmon gate has been designated as a cultural property designated by Tsukuba City.Beside the Zuishinmon gate is the Koyo Shonin Five Ring Tower. This is the only temple-related {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=doto pagoda|1=UNKNOWN}} that remains at Tsukubasan Shrine, which was spared from destruction and relocation during {{ill|lt=Haitbutsu Kishaku|Q702298|WD=Q702298|1=Haibutsu kishaku}} in the early Meiji period. One theory is that Kotan was the son of {{ill|lt=Hidetada Tokugawa|Q314464|WD=Q314464|1=Tokugawa Hidetada}}'s wet nurse, and entered the Betsto Chisoku-in temple on Mt. Tsukuba in 1611. {{ill|lt=Osaka no Siege|Q475780|WD=Q475780|lt=Osaka Summer Siege and Winter Siege|1=Siege of Osaka}} He is said to have served in the army and prayed for victory, and is said to have died on December 19, 1624, {{ill|lt=Kanei|Q1192913|WD=Q1192913|1=Kan'ei}}<ref>{{Cite book|Japanese book|title=Kanto's Famous Mountain, Mt. Tsukuba, Guide to Mt. Tsukuba Shrine|publisher=Mt. Tsukuba Shrine|year=2008|page=40}}</ref>.The ``Shinkyo Bridge'' built on the approach is {{ill|lt=Gabled|Q13308575|WD=Q13308575|1=Kirizuma-style}} with a thatched roof, 1 ken in frontage and 4 ken in depth, and is a sled bridge in the {{ill|lt=Azuchi-Momoyama period|Q319531|WD=Q319531|1=Azuchi–Momoyama period}} style. </ref>{{Sfn|Ibaraki Prefectural Board of Education|Shinbashi}}. It is said to have been donated by the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu in 1633, and was renovated by the fifth shogun {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Tsunayoshi|Q313745|WD=Q313745|1=Tokugawa Tsunayoshi}} in {{ill|lt=Genroku|Q1325001|WD=Q1325001|1=Genroku}}15 ({{ill|lt=1702|Q6819|WD=Q6819|1=1702}}). During the Spring and Autumn Gozachange Festival, mikoshi and attendants cross here {{Sfn|Ibaraki Prefectural Board of Education|Shinbashi}}. Worshipers are normally prohibited from crossing the bridge, and are only allowed during the Spring and Autumn Gozagae Festival and the February Toshikoshi Festival <ref name="Shinkyo Explanation Board"/>. This Shinbashi bridge is designated as a cultural property of Ibaraki Prefecture.

A bronze statue of {{ill|lt=Koshiro Fujita|Q11288247|WD=Q11288247|1=Fujita Kishirō}} is erected near the entrance to the shrine. Koshiro Fujita was the fourth son of {{ill|lt=Toko Fujita|Q6564466|WD=Q6564466|1=Fujita Tōko}} and was the leader of the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Tenguto|1=UNKNOWN}} Tsukuba faction that raised an army at Mt. Tsukuba on March 27, 1864 (May 2, 1864). A plaque by Mito local historians Ohsui Ando and Saburo Yumene was installed near the statue in 1990.Additionally, Kasuga Shrine, Hie Shrine, and Itsukushima Shrine, which were built as guardian shrines of Chuzenji Temple, remain today as the final shrines of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine (see section {{ill|lt=#terminal shrine|UNKNOWN|lt=terminal shrine}}). Near Kasuga Hie Shrine stands a variety of camphor tree {{ill|lt=Marubagus|Q24868661|WD=Q24868661|1=Cinnamomum camphora var. rotundifolia}} (Marubagusu), which is designated as a natural monument by Tsukuba City, and near the tower gate stands a large cedar tree that is about 800 years old and 32 meters tall.

In addition, within the precincts are the grave of {{ill|lt=Masakatsu Kusunoki|Q11541195|WD=Q11541195|1=Kusunoki Masakatsu}}, the grandson of {{ill|lt=Masashige Kusunoki|Q382234|WD=Q382234|1=Kusunoki Masashige}}, and the space egg that was exhibited at the {{ill|lt=International Science and Technology Exposition|Q2312924|WD=Q2312924|1=Expo 85 (Tsukuba, Japan)}} (Science Expo). Although normally closed to the public, there is also a storehouse that collects various materials provided to pray for and express gratitude for the success of each pavilion (it was opened to the public for a limited time in July 2006). Additionally, monuments of 25 Tsukuba poems from the Manyoshu have been erected around Mt. Tsukuba, including Mt. Tsukuba Shrine.
