=== Suesha ===
It is said that before {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=shrine organization|1=UNKNOWN}} there were more than 100 subsidiary shrines. Most of them are still located in the mountains, and the following six shrines are located around the shrine.

* Hie Shrine/Kasuga Shrine
** Hie Shrine enshrined deity: {{ill|lt=Oyama Kuijin|Q10935105|WD=Q10935105|1=Oyamakui no Kami}}
** Deities enshrined at Kasuga Shrine: {{ill|WD=Q1502690|lt=Takemikazuchi|Q1502690|WD=Q1502690|WD=Q1502690|lt=Takemikazuchi-kami|1=Takemikazuchi}}, {{ill|lt=Tunetsu-nushi-kami|Q1107286|WD=Q1107286|1=Futsunushi}}, {{ill|lt=Amagiya-no-Mikoto|Q2564508|WD=Q2564508|lt=Amenoyane-kami|1=Ame-no-Koyane}}, {{ill|lt=Hime-kami|Q22070227|WD=Q22070227|1=Himegami}}
*: Both shrines are located adjacent to the left side of the main hall of worship, and share the hall of worship. To the east is Hie Shrine, and to the west is Kasuga Shrine. The enshrined deity is said to have been enshrined as a guardian shrine at the time of the founding of Chuzenji Temple.<ref name="Hie Kasuga"/> Both the main shrine and the worship hall are said to have been donated by the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu in 1633 (1633).*: The main shrine is a three-way shrine {{ill|lt=Nagazuzukuri|Q210684|WD=Q210684|1=Nagare-zukuri}} that has the same size and structure, and has a width of 2 rooms and a depth of 2 rooms.Hie Shrine and Kasuga Shrine information boards. </ref>. On the toad in the center of the back, a monkey is carved at Hie Shrine and a deer is carved at Kasuga Shrine.{{Sfn|Ibaraki Prefectural Board of Education|Kasuga Shrine Hie Shrine}}. The worship hall is in the Warihaiden style, with 5 ken rows and 2 beams {{ill|lt=Irimoya-zukuri|Q855025|WD=Q855025|1=East Asian hip-and-gable roof}}, with eaves and gables on the front and back <ref name="Hie Kasuga"/>. The main shrine and worship hall of both shrines have been designated as Ibaraki Prefecture's designated cultural properties.
* Itsukushima Shrine
** Enshrined deity: {{ill|lt=Ichikishima Hime|Q11059454|WD=Q11059454|lt=Ichikishima Hime no Mikoto|1=Ichikishima-hime}}
*: Enshrined in the pond southwest of the main hall of worship. The enshrined deity is <ref name="Itsukushima"/>, which is said to be Kansei from {{ill|lt=Lake Biwa|Q200239|WD=Q200239|1=Lake Biwa}}{{ill|lt=Chikubushima|Q2975401|WD=Q2975401|1=Chikubu Island}}. The shrine building is said to have been donated by the third shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu in 1633.<ref name="Itsukushima"/> {{ill|lt=Kasugazukuri|Q3193781|WD=Q3193781|1=Kasuga-zukuri}}<ref name="Itsukushima">Itsukushima Shrine information board with a frontage of 1.5 rooms and a depth of 10 feet. </ref>. A serpent divine messenger is carved on the front of the shrine.{{Sfn|Ibaraki Prefectural Board of Education|Itsukushima Shrine Main Hall}}. This shrine building has been designated as a cultural property by Ibaraki Prefecture.
* Asahi Inari Shrine
** Enshrined deity: {{ill|lt=Ota Mikoto|Q97496337|WD=Q97496337|1=Ōta-no-Mikoto}}*: Another name is ``Shusse Inari.'' It is located behind the main hall of worship. It is said to have been founded by {{ill|lt=Emperor Saga|Q316654|WD=Q316654|1=Emperor Saga}}, the fourth prince of Hitachi Province, {{ill|lt=Imperial Prince Tadayoshi|Q17210943|WD=Q17210943|1=Tadayoshi-shinnō}}.
* Inari Shrine (Inari Shrine)
*: It is located next to Asahi Inari Shrine.
* Atago Shrine (Atagoyama Shrine)
*: Enshrined at the back of the east side of the main hall of worship.
