=== Approach ===
The ancient approach to the shrine is known as ``''{{ill|lt=Ibaraki Prefectural Road No. 139 Tsukubayama Park Line|Q11617553|WD=Q11617553|lt=Tsukuba Road|1=Ibaraki Prefectural Road Route 139}}'''. The road starting from Hojo, Tsukuba City and leading to the shrine ({{Google Map Route Map 2 |1=36.17782412531196,140.09265904155927|2=36.213667,+140.101306|3=@36.2112566,140.0984105,17z|data=!4m10!4m9!1m3!2m 2!1d140.092659!2d36.1778241!1m3!2m2!1d140.101306!2d36.213667!3e2?hl}}), and is currently followed by {{ill|lt=Ibaraki Prefectural Route 139 Tsukubayama Park Line|Q11617553|WD=Q11617553|1=Ibaraki Prefectural Road Route 139}}. The entire section has been selected as one of {{ill|lt=Japan's top 100 roads|Q11506119|WD=Q11506119|1=100 Best Roads in Japan}}. Also, on the way to the shrine, there is a torii gate at 6-chome. This torii gate was built in {{ill|lt=Horeki|Q1194397|WD=Q1194397|1=Hōreki}}9 ({{ill|lt=1759|Q7643|WD=Q7643|1=1759}}), and it is said that it used to have a plaque that read "Tsukuba Shrine, the Creation of Heaven and Earth". </ref>. During the Spring and Autumn Gozagae Festival, there is a shrine ceremony from the worship hall to this point.On the other hand, the current main route is {{ill|lt=Ibaraki Prefectural Route 42 Kasama Tsukuba Line|Q11617758|WD=Q11617758|1=Ibaraki Prefectural Road Route 42}} from {{ill|lt=Kanto Railway Tsukuba Kita Office|Q11602873|WD=Q11602873|lt=Tsukuba Yamaguchi Bus Terminal|1=Tsukuba Yamaguchi Bus Terminal}}. In the past, the {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Station|Q18575588|WD=Q18575588|1=Tsukuba Station}} of the {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Railway Tsukuba Line|Q11602898|WD=Q11602898|1=Tsukuba Line}} was also located at the same bus terminal, and a large torii gate has been built near the station site.

In addition, {{ill|lt=cattail oil|Q11296148|WD=Q11296148|1=Gama-abura}} ({{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=ointment|1=UNKNOWN}}) is known as a specialty of the approach. The story of the cattail oil seller has been certified as a local intangible folk cultural property by Tsukuba City, and preservation efforts are underway. "Preservation Society" in its 15th year] (Tokyo Shimbun, December 12, 2013). </ref>.

