=== Chuzenji ===
'''Chuzenji'' (Chuzenji) is a Muhonji temple {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}} of the {{ill|lt=Shingon sect|Q550182|WD=Q550182|1=Shingon Buddhism}} that existed in the area centered around the worship hall of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine. {{ill|lt=Mountain name|Q11058522|WD=Q11058522|1=sangō}} is Mt. Tsukuba, and the temple name is Chisoku-in. The principal image is {{ill|lt=Senju Kannon|Q10904910|WD=Q10904910|1=Thousand-armed Kannon}}. It was also the 25th temple of {{ill|lt=Bando Thirty-Three Kannon|Q806417|WD=Q806417|1=Bandō Sanjūsankasho}} {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}.

It is said to have started with {{ill|lt=Tokuichi|Q7813728|WD=Q7813728|1=Tokuitsu}}, a monk of the {{ill|lt=Hosso sect|Q349849|WD=Q349849|1=East Asian Yogācāra}}, who opened ``Tsukubasan-dera''. is also found in "{{ill|lt=Genkoshasho|Q4153225|WD=Q4153225|1=Genkō Shakusho}}" of {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|WD=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}} {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. Its founding year is the first year of {{ill|lt=Enryaku|Q1373176|WD=Q1373176|1=Enryaku}} ({{ill|lt=782 years|Q30550|WD=Q30550|1=782}}) <ref name="Yamakawa"/>, Enryaku era (782-{{ill|lt=806|Q30021|WD=Q30021|1=806}}) {{Sfn|Chuzenji ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}, {{ill|lt=Tencho|Q1373138|WD=Q1373138|1=Tenchō}} first year ({{ill|lt=824|Q30353|WD=Q30353|1=824}}) {{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukuba-san Shrine|1984|p=416}}, Tencho era (824-{{ill|lt=834|Q30381|WD=Q30381|1=834}}) {{Sfn|Tsukubasan-jinja Shrine (Kadokawa)|1983}}, etc.With the opening of Tsukuba-san Temple, the two deities of Mt. Tsukuba, male and female, came to be enshrined as ``Mt. Tsukuba Ryobu Gongen,'' with Kannon as the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=real Buddha|1=UNKNOWN}} {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. Since Mt. Tsukuba has been a place for mountain training since ancient times, temples gradually flourished after that, and the name of the temple is said to have come to be called Chuzen-ji ('''Mt. Tsukuba Chisoku-in Chuzen-ji'') {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. {{ill|lt=Middle Ages|Q12554|WD=Q12554|1=Middle Ages}} include Mt. Nikko ({{ill|lt=Rinnoji|Q1348249|WD=Q1348249|1=Rinnō-ji}}), Mt. Sagami Daisen ({{ill|lt=Oyama Afuri Shrine|Q11434713|WD=Q11434713|lt=Daisenji|1=Ōyama Afuri Shrine}}), and Izu Hasashiyu ({{jal Ink|Izusan Shrine|Q611460|Izusan Sansho Gongen}}), {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}} is said to have been one of the leading {{ill|lt=Shugendo|Q1483875|WD=Q1483875|1=Shugendō}} sacred sites in the Kanto region. The charge was held by the Tsukuba clan, starting with Tameji Tsukuba (Myogen) {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}.The situation in the Middle Ages is not known, but in the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|WD=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, it was protected by the shogunate as a place of prayer for {{ill|lt=Kimon|Q11673539|WD=Q11673539|1=Kimon}}, and the temple flourished again {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Ieyasu|Q171977|WD=Q171977|1=Tokugawa Ieyasu}} abolished the Tsukuba clan from the Tsukuba mountain betto in {{ill|lt=Keicho|Q1328185|WD=Q1328185|1=Keichō}} 5th year ({{ill|lt=1600|Q6720|WD=Q6720|1=1600}}), appointed Yutoshi as the founder of the Chukyo era, and established the Keicho era. In 1602|Q6727}}, he donated 500 koku as a divine territory, and in 15 Keicho years ({{ill|lt=1610|Q6765|WD=Q6765|1=1610}}), he donated 500 koku as a temple territory. In addition, the third shogun {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Iemitsu|Q314481|WD=Q314481|1=Tokugawa Iemitsu}} restored the two shrines on the mountaintop, and also built the main hall (Omido), three-storied pagoda, bell tower, tower gate, Shinkyo Bridge, and shrines within the precincts of Hie, Kasuga, and Itsukushima {{Sfn|Ruins of Chuzenji Temple (Heibonsha)|1982}}. Also, during the time of the fifth shogun {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Tsunayoshi|Q313745|WD=Q313745|1=Tokugawa Tsunayoshi}}, it was renamed ``''Gojiin'''', and the temple territory counted 1,500 koku {{Sfn|Mt. Tsukuba Shrine (Kadokawa)|1983}}. After that, it continued to develop as a sacred mountain throughout the Edo period, and the {{ill|lt=monzen town|Q10564515|WD=Q10564515|1=Monzen-machi}} also developed {{Sfn|Chuzenji Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}.After the Meiji Restoration, due to the {{ill|lt=Haibutsu Kishaku|Q702298|WD=Q702298|1=Haibutsu kishaku}}, the functions of Chuzenji ceased, the halls and pagodas were destroyed except for some shrine buildings, and the ritual implements were scattered all over the place {{Sfn|Ruins of Chuzenji Temple (Heibonsha)|1982}}. In 1930 (1930|Q18787}}), '''{{ill|lt=Omido (Tsukuba City)|Q11435427|WD=Q11435427|lt=Omido|1=Ōmi-dō}}''' (Omido) was rebuilt as a temple of the {{ill|lt=Shingon sect Toyozan school|Q3077351|WD=Q3077351|1=Shingon-shū Buzan-ha}} to the southwest of the worship hall of Mt. Tsukuba Shrine, and it continues to this day.

