=== Ruins of Rokusho Shrine ===
: Located in Tsukuba City {{ill|lt=Usui (Tsukuba City)|Q17194126|WD=Q17194126|lt=Usui|1=Usui, Tsukuba}} ({{Coord|36|12|2.18|N|140|6|38.53|E|region:JP-08|name=Motosato Shrine Ruins of Rokusho Shrine}}).
: Also known as ``Rokusho Kodaijingu Ruins.'' The site of Satomiya, now a closed shrine. ``Rokusho'' is a general term for the two shrines of Tsukuba's male and female gods and the four shrines of Setsusha {{Sfn|Rokusho Shrine Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}. It is said that in the past, there were two shrines dedicated to the male and female deities, a worship hall, a Zuishinmon gate, and a treasure trove.{{Sfn|Rokusho Shrine Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}} This Rokusho Shrine was closed in {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|WD=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}43 ({{ill|lt=1910|Q2075|WD=Q2075|1=1910}}), and the divine spirit was (Tsukuba City)|Q17209343|Kamigun}} It was enshrined in the {{ill|lt=Kakekage Shrine|Q17228464|WD=Q17228464|1=Kokage Shrine}} (Kakekageyama Shrine) in the {{Sfn|Rokusho Shrine Ruins (Heibonsha)|1982}}.
: According to "Silk Shadow Mountain Shintokuki", in the 20th year of {{ill|lt=Enryaku|Q1373176|WD=Q1373176|1=Enryaku}} ({{ill|lt=801|Q30008|WD=Q30008|1=801}}), k|Sakanoue Tamuramaro|Q941979}} is said to have deposited horse harnesses, a treasured sword, and a divine mirror here on his way back from the Ezo conquest. {{Sfn|Ruins of Rokusho Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}. When the torii gate collapsed in the 3rd year of the Meiji era ({{ill|lt=1870|Q7741|WD=Q7741|1=1870}}), a bronze mirror was unearthed, and it is said that there was an inscription on the mirror that read, ``Stone torii gate, Seii Taishogun Sakanoue Tamuramaro erected'' {{Sfn|Ruins of Rokusho Shrine (Heibonsha)|1982}}.

=== {{ill|lt=Hachimanzuka Tumulus (Tsukuba City)|Q17218702|WD=Q17218702|lt=Hachimanzuka Tumulus|1=Hachimanzuka-kofun}} ===
: Located in Numata, Tsukuba City ({{Coord|36|12|23.54|N|140|4|41.24|E|region:JP-08|name=Hachimanzuka Tumulus}}).
: Located at the southwest foot of Mt. Tsukuba, this is the largest {{ill|lt=kofun|Q1141225|WD=Q1141225|1=Kofun}} in the Tsukuba region. This is a {{ill|lt=keyhole-shaped tumulus|Q11268718|WD=Q11268718|1=Zenpō-kōen-fun}} with a length of 90 meters, and is thought to have been constructed in the first half of the {{ill|lt=6th century|Q8090|WD=Q8090|1=6th century}}. </ref>. Abeshiko no Mikoto of the first generation {{ill|lt=Tsukuba Kunizo|Q11602846|WD=Q11602846|1=Tsukuba Kunizo}}<ref group="Note" name="Kunizo">About Tsukuba Kunizo, "{{ill|lt=Hitachikuni Fudoki|Q11481687|WD=Q11481687|1=Hitachi no Kuni Fudoki}}" In Tsukuba Gunjo, Tsukuba no Mikoto (Tsukuba Mikoto) was the reign of the 10th {{ill|lt=Emperor Sujin|Q333530|WD=Q333530|1=Emperor Sujin}}. He was dispatched to ``{{ill|lt=Sendai Kūjihonki|Q2716751|WD=Q2716751|1=Kujiki}}'' ``Kokuzohonki'' In Tsukuba Kunizojo, Akoshiiro no Mikoto, the grandson of Ninko Takami no Mikoto, was appointed as the successor of the 13th Emperor, {{ill|lt=Emperor Seimu|Q316672|WD=Q316672|1=Emperor Seimu}}. Regarding this, there is a theory that the genealogy goes as ``Oshinobu no Mikoto - Chikutan no Mikoto - Akuseiro no Mikoto'', and another theory that Chikutan no Mikoto was a pioneer and the first generation was Akuseiro no Mikoto {{Harv|Tsukuba Province (Kadokawa)|1983}}. It is said to be the tomb of Mt. Tsukuba, and its relationship with the rituals of Mt. Tsukuba has been pointed out {{Sfn|Kitabatake Tsukubasan Shrine|1984|p=414}}. There are many other ancient tombs in addition to this one, located at the southern foot of Mt. Tsukuba.{{Sfn|Ibaraki Prefectural Board of Education|Yawatazuka}}

